    feeds: ["Longevity protection", "Decision quality"],
    description:
      "Economic theory says people facing uncertain lifespans should value lifetime income, yet few annuitize. Experiments show the decision flips with framing: when described as guaranteed monthly spending for life, most people prefer the annuity; when described as an investment with a risky return, most reject it. Pricing studies find annuities offer reasonable value for the average buyer, and partial annuitization and inflation protection make them more appealing. The shelf does not recommend a product; it describes how to evaluate one so the frame does not decide for you.",
    action: "When offered a pension lump sum or considering an annuity, write out both options as monthly spending for the rest of your life (not as a pile of money), compare that against your essential expenses, and consider covering only the essentials with guaranteed income; get an independent fee-only review before signing.",
