    description:
      "Theory established that with capital-gains taxes on realization, the optimal policy is to realize losses promptly and defer gains, and dynamic models quantify how much this adds to after-tax wealth under trading costs and wash-sale-style constraints. Empirical work on investment taxation shows taxes materially reduce realized portfolio performance, which is what harvesting partially recovers. The honest boundary: benefits are modest, largest for high-bracket investors with volatile taxable holdings, zero inside IRAs and 401(k)s, and can be undone by re-buying the same security within the wash-sale window.",
    action: "If you hold investments in a taxable account, once a year (or after a market drop) review positions below cost, sell the losers and immediately buy a similar but not identical fund, and record the realized loss for your return; never do this inside a retirement account.",
